 "findings": [
  {
   "evidence": "Naming conventions: \"When referring to a namespaced definition in a lemma name ... This is to ensure that `_`-separated strings in a lemma name correspond to a definition name or connective.\" No declaration is named `divPow`; grep shows only `sphericalBesselJDivPow` (line 105). Lines 208 and 360 are `deriv_sphericalBesselJDivPow` and `deriv_sphericalBesselJ_divPow`.",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Mathematics/SpecialFunctions/SphericalBessel/Basic.lean",
   "fix": "Drop `_divPow`: give the whole-line statements the base names (`hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJ` / `deriv_sphericalBesselJ`, `..._succ`) and mark the restricted ones by their hypothesis, `hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJ_of_ne_zero` / `deriv_sphericalBesselJ_of_ne_zero` (currently lines 418, 435). That removes the case-only distinction and stops a name from describing the proof. Rename the private helpers `analyticOnNhd_divPow` (143), `deriv_divPow_zero` (190), `divPow_one` (219), `divPow_recurrence_zero` (225) to spell the definition out too, since `divPow` is not a declaration name.",
   "issue": "`deriv_sphericalBesselJ_divPow` and `deriv_sphericalBesselJDivPow` (line 208) differ only by the case of one letter and an underscore, but state different things: the first is `deriv (sphericalBesselJ l)`, the second `deriv (sphericalBesselJDivPow l)`. Read as a name, the segment `_divPow` points at `sphericalBesselJDivPow` \u2014 i.e. the wrong subject; what it actually encodes is the proof route (the docstring: \"by the product rule for `j_l(x) = x ^ l * sphericalBesselJDivPow l x`\"). Same defect at `hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJ_divPow` (351) vs `hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJDivPow` (195), and in `hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJ_succ_divPow` (373) / `deriv_sphericalBesselJ_succ_divPow` (386).",
   "line": 360
  }
 ],
